{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794606239,"nonce":"2yuS9KNuvBFE4tMxoLxFFtZkp38=","message":"Three commits since the last note, all local and not pushed: 12dbd2e5, bf070ca5 and 12dbd751. The last one replaces the release step from 9ec6c1f8, so the earlier comment about localize_runtime.sh no longer describes the code.\n\n12dbd2e5: an Android program defines `xwindow_main`, which hlwindow's `android_main` calls on the activity's thread. hlwindow's Android archive links with no stub.\n\nbf070ca5: `gc_register_current_os_thread` handled Linux, macOS, Windows and wasm. Android and iOS fell through to a guessed stack top of `sp + 1 MB` for every non-main thread, which runs into the guard page of a bionic thread stack. hxn reproduced it with plain Haxe: a major collection with a live thread exited 139. With the real pthread stack boundary the same program exits 0 on the emulator (3 of 3; it crashed 3 of 3 before) and on the iOS simulator.\n\n12dbd751, replacing the release step: `scripts/localize_runtime.sh` used `nmedit`, which leaves an undefined reference to `_rust_eh_personality` beside the local definition. The archive linked unstripped and failed once `strip -S` ran, and the release strips. The script and its two release steps are removed. ash now isolates the Rust internals when it links. Each Rust archive named with `--native-lib`, and the runtime when it exports the same internals, is merged into one object that exports only its C interface. On Apple that is `clang -r` with `-all_load` and an export list; on Android `clang -r` with `--whole-archive`, then `llvm-objcopy --keep-global-symbols`. The driver's own `-r` is needed on Android, because the NDK driver otherwise adds `-pie`, which lld rejects with a relocatable output (hxn hit this). Symbols are read with the `object` crate, because Apple's `nm` cannot read the bitcode that a plain `rustc --crate-type staticlib` leaves in its members.\n\nTested: link tests with two small Rust staticlibs beside the unprocessed runtime on iOS and Android, and the real hlwindow archives with stripped, release-style runtimes, which print the expected output on the simulator and the emulator.\n\nAlso in 12dbd751: a library primitive with the same name as an ash_std export is refused at build time, naming both. hxn's `future_state` against `hlp_future_state` is the case; before, it was a duplicate-symbol error, or a silent mis-binding when the library did not define the resolver, because the weak reference resolved to ash_std's plain function and ash ran it as a resolver. Libraries should prefix primitive names with the library, as HashLink's do.\n\niOS program model: decided on a fiber, after asking Ashui. Their loop (`WindowManager.run`) already has one blocking call, `window.wait`, and needs no change. xwindow 9c76df3 and hlwindow 19f3a59 are pushed: `xwindow_set_turn_yield` lets a host suspend the program when `wait` or `poll` finds nothing. The ash side is not built: a program fiber on the main scheduler (`spawn` runs a new fiber immediately, which would happen before the first turn, so it needs an install-without-run variant), a turn function that calls `schedule_step`, and an iOS `main` that passes both to hlwindow. Testing it needs an app bundle on the simulator, because `simctl spawn` has no UIKit application.\n\nStill untested: a different rustc for hlwindow than for the runtime, hlwindow and hlwgpu in one app, Android trace frame names, and the iOS device.","files":null}]}